Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional affine Poisson manifold equipped with a Poisson bi-vector $P$, so that the Schouten bracket $[\![P,P]\!]$ vanishes. Let $\partial_P = [\![P,\cdot]\!]$ denote the Poisson coboundary on multivector fields. An infinitesimal symmetry of the Poisson structure is a multivector field $Q(P)$, depending on $P$, satisfying $[\![P,Q(P)]\!] = 0$ modulo terms proportional to $[\![P,P]\!]$. Such a symmetry is called:

- trivial in Poisson cohomology if $Q(P) = [\![P,X(P)]\!]$ for some vector field $X(P)$, again up to terms proportional to $[\![P,P]\!]$;
- nonlinear if its expression in terms of $P$ is of degree $\geq 2$;
- proper if it is not the restriction of a known universal construction giving a trivial class in a generic situation.

(I) Determine whether there exists an infinitesimal symmetry $Q(P)$ of a Poisson bi-vector $P$ on some finite-dimensional affine Poisson manifold which is simultaneously nonlinear, proper, and $\partial_P$-nontrivial.

(II) If no such $Q(P)$ exists, identify the mechanism that forces $\partial_P$-triviality of all universal Poisson cocycles produced from $\partial_P$-nontrivial cocycles, in particular those obtained by applying the graph orientation morphism $\mathcal{O}\to r$ to nontrivial cocycles $\gamma \in \ker d$ in the Kontsevich graph complex.

